      "traceTextDiagram": "Actor System Supervisor Startup\n├── runActorSysSupervisorStart() \u003c-- cmd/agentctl/cmd/actorsys.go:189\n│   ├── Open mailbox store \u003c-- cmd/agentctl/cmd/actorsys.go:206\n│   ├── Create actor system \u003c-- cmd/agentctl/cmd/actorsys.go:230\n│   ├── Load hook configuration \u003c-- 1a\n│   │   └── hooks.LoadConfigWithDefaults() \u003c-- internal/hooks/config.go:111\n│   │       ├── Resolve config paths \u003c-- 1b\n│   │       │   └── DefaultConfigPaths() \u003c-- internal/hooks/config.go:49\n│   │       │       ├── workspace/.agentctl/hooks.yaml \u003c-- internal/hooks/config.go:54\n│   │       │       └── ~/.agentctl/hooks.yaml \u003c-- internal/hooks/config.go:60\n│   │       └── LoadConfig(paths...) \u003c-- internal/hooks/config.go:68\n│   │           └── for each path \u003c-- internal/hooks/config.go:77\n│   │               ├── loadConfigFile() \u003c-- 1c\n│   │               │   ├── yaml.Unmarshal() \u003c-- internal/hooks/config.go:128\n│   │               │   └── toHookDef() \u003c-- 1d\n│   │               │       └── Apply defaults \u003c-- internal/hooks/config.go:242\n│   │               └── Merge hooks by ID \u003c-- internal/hooks/config.go:93\n│   ├── Build event index \u003c-- 1e\n│   │   └── merged.buildIndex() \u003c-- internal/hooks/config.go:31\n│   │       └── map[Event][]HookDef sorted \u003c-- internal/hooks/config.go:40\n│   └── Create dispatcher \u003c-- 1f\n│       └── NewDispatcherWithRegistry() \u003c-- internal/hooks/registry.go:125\n│           ├── NewRegistry() \u003c-- internal/hooks/registry.go:37\n│           │   └── SkillRunner + ShellRunner \u003c-- internal/hooks/registry.go:59\n│           └── NewDispatcher() \u003c-- internal/hooks/dispatcher.go:114",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nAgentctl's hook system allows **runtime interception and control** of agent operations at canonical lifecycle events. The problem: agents need to enforce policies (like preventing file conflicts between multiple agents), inject context (like task information), and observe operations (like tracking tool usage) **without hardcoding these concerns into the core agent logic**. Hooks provide a **declarative, configuration-driven** solution where users define YAML rules that match events and execute skills to approve, block, or augment operations.\n\n## Details\n\n### Configuration Loading\n\nAt system startup, the supervisor loads hook definitions from **two locations in precedence order**: workspace-local `.agentctl/hooks.yaml` and global `~/.agentctl/hooks.yaml` [1a]. The `DefaultConfigPaths` function resolves these paths [1b], with workspace config taking precedence to allow per-project customization.\n\nEach YAML file is parsed by `loadConfigFile` [1c], which unmarshals hook definitions and applies defaults for timeout, fail_open behavior, and enabled status [1d]. The `toHookDef` function handles this normalization, resolving fields like `fail_mode: \"open\"` to boolean `fail_open: true` and applying global defaults from the `defaults:` section.\n\n### Event Index Optimization\n\nAfter loading, `buildIndex` creates a **fast lookup map** from event types to matching hooks [1e]. This map structure (`map[Event][]HookDef`) enables **O(1) event-to-hooks lookup** during dispatch, avoiding linear scans through all hooks. Hooks are sorted by priority within each event bucket, ensuring deterministic execution order.\n\n### Dispatcher Creation\n\nThe system creates a dispatcher with an integrated registry [1f] that routes hook execution to the appropriate runner type. The `NewDispatcherWithRegistry` function combines:\n- **Config**: The merged hook definitions with event index\n- **Registry**: Routes to `SkillRunner` (for Go binaries) or `ShellRunner` (for .sh scripts)\n- **Skills directory**: Path to installed hook skills like `hooks/file_guard`\n\nThis dispatcher is then injected into agent actors, making hooks available at all canonical events (SessionStart, PreToolUse, PostToolUse, etc.)."
    },
