      "traceTextDiagram": "PreToolUse Event Dispatch Flow\n├── AgentActor.dispatchHook() \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:1107\n│   ├── buildHookInput() - prepare context \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:1112\n│   └── hooks.Dispatch(ctx, input) \u003c-- 2a\n│       └── Dispatcher.Dispatch() \u003c-- internal/hooks/dispatcher.go:122\n│           ├── HooksForEvent(input.Event) \u003c-- 2b\n│           │   └── return byEvent[event] (indexed) \u003c-- internal/hooks/config.go:27\n│           ├── Filter by matchers \u003c-- internal/hooks/dispatcher.go:137\n│           │   ├── MatchesInput(h, input) \u003c-- 2c\n│           │   │   ├── matchRegex(ToolName) \u003c-- 2d\n│           │   │   ├── matchRegex(PathRegex) \u003c-- internal/hooks/dispatcher.go:305\n│           │   │   │   └── extractFilePath() \u003c-- 2e\n│           │   │   └── other matchers...\n│           │   └── collect matchingHooks \u003c-- internal/hooks/dispatcher.go:139\n│           ├── Execute each hook \u003c-- internal/hooks/dispatcher.go:150\n│           │   └── runner.Run(hookCtx, hook) \u003c-- 2f\n│           └── Merge(outputs) \u003c-- 2g\n│               └── return Result \u003c-- internal/hooks/dispatcher.go:210",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nAgentctl's hook system solves a critical problem: **how do you intercept and control agent operations at key lifecycle points without hardcoding policy logic into the core runtime?** When an AI agent attempts to edit a file, execute a command, or complete a turn, you need the ability to:\n\n- **Block dangerous operations** before they execute (e.g., prevent editing files reserved by other agents)\n- **Inject context** into the agent's prompt (e.g., add task information or warnings)\n- **Mutate tool inputs** (e.g., sanitize paths or add flags)\n- **Observe and log** operations for debugging and compliance\n\nThe hook system provides this through **event-driven dispatch**: at canonical events like `PreToolUse`, `PostToolUse`, or `SessionStart`, the runtime dispatches to configured hooks that can approve, block, or modify the operation.\n\n## Details\n\n### Configuration and Loading\n\nHook definitions live in YAML files at `.agentctl/hooks.yaml` (workspace-level) or `~/.agentctl/hooks.yaml` (global). At system startup [1a], the supervisor loads these configs [1b], which define:\n\n- **Event type** (e.g., `PreToolUse`, `PostAgentTurn`)\n- **Matchers** (regex patterns for tool names, file paths, actor IDs)\n- **Skills to run** (e.g., `hooks/file_guard`, `hooks/task_guard`)\n- **Execution policy** (timeout, fail-open vs fail-closed)\n\nThe config system builds an **event index** [1e] mapping each event type to its hooks, sorted by priority for fast O(1) lookup.\n\n### Dispatch Flow\n\nWhen an event occurs (e.g., agent about to use a tool), the actor calls `Dispatch()` [2a] with a `hooks.Input` containing event context (tool name, file path, session ID, etc.).\n\nThe dispatcher:\n1. **Looks up hooks** for the event type using the pre-built index [2b]\n2. **Filters by matchers** [2c] - each hook can specify regex patterns for tool names [2d], file paths [2e], actor IDs, etc. Only hooks where **all matchers pass** (AND logic) are executed\n3. **Executes matching hooks** sequentially in priority order [2f]\n4. **Merges outputs** [2g] using deterministic rules (block wins, last-wins for mutations, contexts joined with newlines)\n\n### Matching Logic\n\nThe `MatchesInput()` function [2c] implements the filtering logic. For a hook to run, **all specified matchers must match**:\n\n- **Tool name matching** [2d]: Regex against `tool_name` (e.g., `Edit`, `Write`) or `tool_canonical` (e.g., `edit.apply_patch`)\n- **Path matching** [2e]: Extracts file paths from tool input JSON (checks `file_path`, `path`, `current_path` fields) and matches against `path_regex`\n- **Actor/namespace matching**: Regex against `actor_id` or mailbox namespace\n- **Tool kind matching**: Category-based matching (read/write/exec/search)\n\nRegex patterns are **compiled and cached** for performance to avoid recompilation on every dispatch.\n\n### Execution and Output\n\nHooks are executed via **skill-backed runners** [2f] - Go binaries that receive `hooks.Input` on stdin as JSON and return `hooks.Output` on stdout. The output specifies:\n\n- **Decision**: `approve`, `block`, or `none` (advisory)\n- **Reason**: Human-readable explanation\n- **Context**: Markdown text to inject into agent prompt\n- **Actions**: Structured actions like `enqueue_context`, `send_mailbox`, `run_skill`\n- **Mutations**: `updated_tool_input` or `updated_assistant_text` to modify the operation\n\n### Merge Rules\n\nWhen multiple hooks run [2g], their outputs are merged deterministically:\n\n- **Decision**: Block wins over approve; approve wins over none\n- **Reason**: First non-empty reason for the winning decision\n- **Mutations**: Last-wins (later hooks override earlier ones)\n- **Context**: All contexts joined with `\\n\\n`\n- **Actions**: Concatenated in execution order\n\nThis ensures predictable behavior when multiple hooks interact."
    },
