      "traceTextDiagram": "Tool Execution with Hook Integration\n├── LLMChatEngine.Run() \u003c-- internal/engine/llmchat_engine.go:116\n│   └── for each tool call \u003c-- internal/engine/llmchat_engine.go:166\n│       └── toolRunner.Execute() \u003c-- 4a\n│           ├── dispatchPreToolUse() \u003c-- 4b\n│           │   └── dispatcher.Dispatch() \u003c-- internal/engine/tool_runner.go:150\n│           │       └── returns Output with decision \u003c-- internal/engine/tool_runner.go:151\n│           ├── check if blocked \u003c-- 4c\n│           ├── apply input mutation \u003c-- 4d\n│           ├── executor.Execute() \u003c-- 4e\n│           │   └── actual tool runs \u003c-- internal/engine/tool_runner.go:248\n│           └── dispatchPostToolUse() \u003c-- 4f\n│               └── dispatcher.Dispatch() \u003c-- internal/engine/tool_runner.go:185\n│                   └── returns Output for observability \u003c-- internal/engine/tool_runner.go:186\n└── returns ToolResult to engine \u003c-- internal/engine/tool_runner.go:129",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen an LLM agent wants to execute a tool (like editing a file or running a command), the system needs a way to **intercept that execution** to enforce policies, check permissions, or inject context. Without hooks, there's no clean way to prevent an agent from overwriting a file another agent is editing, or to log what tools are being used. The **ToolRunner** solves this by wrapping every tool execution with **PreToolUse** and **PostToolUse** hook dispatch points [4b, 4f], allowing hook skills to approve, block, or modify operations before they happen.\n\n## Details\n\n**Hook dispatch happens in two phases around tool execution.** When the LLM engine needs to run a tool, it calls `toolRunner.Execute()` [4a] which orchestrates the flow. First, it dispatches a **PreToolUse** event [4b] to the hook system, passing the tool name, input arguments, and session context. The dispatcher finds matching hooks and executes them, returning an `Output` with a decision.\n\n**Hooks can block operations before they execute.** The runner checks if any hook returned a blocking decision [4c]. If blocked, it immediately returns an error result without executing the tool. This is how skills like `hooks/file_guard` prevent conflicting file edits between agents.\n\n**Hooks can mutate tool inputs.** If hooks provide `UpdatedToolInput` in their output, the runner applies this mutation [4d] using last-wins semantics. This allows hooks to modify arguments before the tool runs, like redirecting file paths or sanitizing inputs.\n\n**The actual tool executes only after approval.** Once PreToolUse hooks approve (or if no hooks matched), the runner calls `executor.Execute()` [4e] to run the actual tool implementation. This might be a file edit, bash command, or any other tool the agent has access to.\n\n**PostToolUse hooks observe results.** After execution completes, the runner dispatches a **PostToolUse** event [4f] with the tool's output, any errors, and execution duration. These hooks can't block (the tool already ran), but they can log metrics, inject context about what happened, or trigger follow-up actions. The final `ToolResult` is returned to the engine [4a].\n\n**This pattern enables policy enforcement without modifying tool implementations.** Tools remain simple and focused, while hooks handle cross-cutting concerns like conflict detection, auditing, and context injection."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "title": "Hook Output Merging with Decision Priority",
      "description": "Merge subsystem - combines multiple hook outputs using deterministic rules for decisions, mutations, and actions",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "5a",
          "path": "internal/hooks/dispatcher.go",
          "lineNumber": 206,
          "lineContent": "result.Output = Merge(outputs)",
          "title": "Merge outputs from all hooks",
          "description": "Dispatcher calls Merge after collecting outputs from all executed hooks"
        },
        {
          "id": "5b",
          "path": "internal/hooks/merge.go",
          "lineNumber": 40,
          "lineContent": "case DecisionBlock:",
          "title": "Track blocking decisions",
          "description": "Block decision takes highest priority - sets anyBlock flag and captures first block reason"
        },
        {
          "id": "5c",
          "path": "internal/hooks/merge.go",
          "lineNumber": 53,
          "lineContent": "if len(out.UpdatedToolInput) \u003e 0 {",
          "title": "Apply last-wins for tool input",
          "description": "Later hooks override earlier hooks for updated_tool_input mutations"
        },
        {
          "id": "5d",
          "path": "internal/hooks/merge.go",
          "lineNumber": 64,
          "lineContent": "contexts = append(contexts, out.Context)",
          "title": "Collect context strings",
          "description": "Accumulates non-empty context strings from all hooks for joining"
        },
        {
          "id": "5e",
          "path": "internal/hooks/merge.go",
          "lineNumber": 68,
          "lineContent": "allActions = append(allActions, out.Actions...)",
          "title": "Concatenate actions in order",
          "description": "Preserves hook execution order when combining action lists"
        },
        {
          "id": "5f",
          "path": "internal/hooks/merge.go",
          "lineNumber": 78,
          "lineContent": "result.Context = strings.Join(contexts, \"\\n\\n\")",
          "title": "Join contexts with separator",
          "description": "Combines all context strings with double newline separator for readability"
        },
        {
          "id": "5g",
          "path": "internal/hooks/merge.go",
          "lineNumber": 81,
          "lineContent": "if anyBlock {",
          "title": "Apply decision priority rules",
          "description": "Block wins over approve/none; approve wins over none; uses first reason for winning decision"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Hook Output Merging Flow\n└── Dispatcher.Dispatch() \u003c-- internal/hooks/dispatcher.go:122\n    ├── Execute all matching hooks \u003c-- internal/hooks/dispatcher.go:150\n    │   └── Collect outputs from each hook \u003c-- internal/hooks/dispatcher.go:195\n    └── Merge all outputs \u003c-- 5a\n        ├── Iterate through outputs \u003c-- internal/hooks/merge.go:37\n        │   ├── Track blocking decisions \u003c-- 5b\n        │   ├── Apply last-wins mutations \u003c-- 5c\n        │   ├── Collect context strings \u003c-- 5d\n        │   ├── Concatenate actions \u003c-- 5e\n        │   └── Merge metadata (last-wins) \u003c-- internal/hooks/merge.go:71\n        ├── Join contexts with separator \u003c-- 5f\n        └── Apply decision priority rules \u003c-- 5g\n            ├── Block wins over all \u003c-- internal/hooks/merge.go:81\n            ├── Approve wins over none \u003c-- internal/hooks/merge.go:84\n            └── Return merged Output \u003c-- internal/hooks/merge.go:94",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen multiple hooks execute in response to a single event (e.g., PreToolUse), each hook returns its own `Output` containing a decision (approve/block/none), optional context to inject, tool input mutations, and actions to perform. The system needs **deterministic rules** to combine these potentially conflicting outputs into a single coherent result. Without proper merging, you'd have ambiguity: what happens when one hook approves and another blocks? Which context gets injected? The merge logic [5a] solves this by applying strict priority rules that ensure predictable behavior regardless of hook execution order.\n\n## Details\n\n### Decision Priority\n\nThe merge algorithm implements a **strict hierarchy** [5g]: block decisions always win, approve beats none, and none is the default. When iterating through outputs [5b], the first blocking hook's reason is captured and becomes the final reason. This ensures that **any hook can veto an operation**, which is critical for security and policy enforcement hooks like file guards or task guards.\n\n### Mutation Semantics\n\nFor fields like `UpdatedToolInput` and `UpdatedAssistantText`, the system uses **last-wins semantics** [5c]. Later hooks in execution order override earlier ones. This allows hooks to build on each other's transformations - for example, one hook might normalize a file path, and a subsequent hook might add validation parameters.\n\n### Context and Actions\n\nContext strings from all hooks are **concatenated with double newlines** [5d, 5f], preserving all information for the LLM. Actions are **concatenated in execution order** [5e], maintaining the sequence in which hooks want operations performed. Metadata uses shallow merge with last-wins per key, allowing hooks to add debugging information without conflicts.\n\n### Integration Point\n\nThe dispatcher calls `Merge(outputs)` [5a] after executing all matching hooks [2g], before returning the final `Result` to the caller. This ensures the actor or tool runner receives a single, unambiguous decision about whether to proceed, what context to inject, and what actions to execute."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "title": "Example Hook Skill: File Guard with Conflict Detection",
      "description": "Hook skill implementation - demonstrates PreToolUse hook that checks file reservations and blocks/warns on conflicts",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "6a",
          "path": "skills/hooks_file_guard/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 44,
          "lineContent": "func run(ctx context.Context, rc *skillmain.RunContext, in hooks.Input) error {",
          "title": "Hook skill entry point",
          "description": "Skill receives deserialized hooks.Input with event context and tool information"
        },
        {
          "id": "6b",
          "path": "skills/hooks_file_guard/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 49,
          "lineContent": "if !toolutil.IsWriteOperation(in.ToolName, in.ToolCanonical, string(in.ToolKind)) {",
          "title": "Filter non-write operations",
          "description": "Uses cross-platform tool classifier to skip read/search operations"
        },
        {
          "id": "6c",
          "path": "skills/hooks_file_guard/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 86,
          "lineContent": "filePath := pathutil.ExtractPath(in.ToolInput)",
          "title": "Extract file path from tool input",
